   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-bc",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.16",
    "topic_title": "Working with the Intermediate Value Theorem",
    "stem": "Let f be a polynomial with f(1) = 2 and f(5) = 8. Which statement is guaranteed by the Intermediate Value Theorem?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "There is at least one c in (1,5) such that f(c) = 5.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Polynomials are continuous on [1,5], and 2 < 5 < 8. The Intermediate Value Theorem therefore guarantees at least one c in (1,5) with f(c) = 5."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "There is exactly one c in (1,5) such that f(c) = 5.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Existence-versus-uniqueness error: the theorem guarantees that f reaches 5, but a polynomial can reach that value more than once. Fix: Check whether the theorem guarantees existence or also guarantees uniqueness."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "f(3) = 5.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Midpoint interpolation error: averaging the endpoint inputs and outputs assumes a linear relationship that is not given. The theorem guarantees an input where f equals 5, not its exact location. Fix: Distinguish an existence conclusion from a claim about a specific input."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "There is at least one c in (1,5) such that f(c) = 10.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Target-value condition error: 10 lies outside [2,8], so the given endpoint values do not guarantee that f reaches 10 inside the interval. Fix: Compare the target value with both endpoint outputs before applying the theorem."
     }
    ]
   },
